\begin{align*}
\frac{2v_1+v_2}{5} &= \frac{38}{5}, \\
\frac{4v_1+3v_2}{13} &= \frac{58}{13}.
\end{align*}
Simplifying, we get
\begin{align*}
2v_1 + v_2 &= 38, \\
4v_1 + 3v_2 &= 58.
\end{align*}
Subtracting the second equation from the first gives $2v_2 = 24$, or $v_2 = 12$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $2v_1 + 12 = 38$, so $2v_1 = 26$, and $v_1 = 13$. Therefore, $\mathbf{v} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix}}$. <answer>$\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix}$</answer>

